@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@email = amr@example.com
@password = password123

### THE RATING COPY  ·  the event path, end to end
### Not a resource — the ARROW: a write in review-service becoming a column in
### book-service, via RabbitMQ.
###
### No single request proves this works. The cause is here (POST /reviews), the
### effect is in ANOTHER service database, and it lands asynchronously. Every
### check below is two requests with a gap, and the gap is the subject.
###
### PRE-FLIGHT
###   docker compose ps
###   docker exec rabbitmq rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready consumers
###     expect:  reviews  0  1     <- consumers=0 means NOTHING below works
###   UI: http://localhost:15672  (guest/guest)
###
### USE A SEEDED BOOK FOR THE RANKING CHECKS. A book created here is DRAFT and
### GET /books serves PUBLISHED only, so it will never appear in ?sort=top-rated.



### Log in
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###

@accessToken = {{login.response.body.data.tokens.accessToken}}


### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PART 1 · Does the arrow exist at all?
### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### A book to review
# @name createBook
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{ "title": "The Window", "description": "A book whose rating we will watch move.", "genre": ["non-fiction"] }

###

@bookId = {{createBook.response.body.data.id}}


### Baseline  →  reviewsAvgRating: null, reviewsCount: 0
### null is NOT 0. Nobody has reviewed this; that is different from everyone
### hating it. Check the body says so structurally.
# @name bookBefore
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}


### Post a review. NOTE `data.createdAt` IN THE RESPONSE — that is T0,
### the moment the review committed in reviews_db.
# @name createReview
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "bookId": "{{bookId}}", "rating": 5, "comment": "Held up under partition." }

###

@reviewId = {{createReview.response.body.data.id}}


### Read the book back IMMEDIATELY.
### Send this as fast as you can after the POST. Whether the rating has
### already moved is itself the measurement — if it has, your window is
### smaller than your reflexes.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}


### Read it again, unhurried  →  reviewsAvgRating: 5, reviewsCount: 1
### If this one has not moved either, the arrow is broken. Check:
###   docker exec rabbitmq rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready consumers
###   docker compose logs book-service --tail 40
# @name bookAfter
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}

### ─── THE WINDOW ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
### W = bookAfter.reviewsLastRefresh − createReview.createdAt
###
### If reviewsLastRefresh is absent from the response, it is not in
### BookSchema and Fastify stripped it. Either add it there, or read the
### column directly:
###
###   docker exec -it postgres psql -U postgres -d books_db -c \
###     "select id, \"reviewsAvgRating\", \"reviewsCount\", \"reviewsLastRefresh\" \
###      from \"Book\" where id = '<bookId>';"
###
### DO NOT use now() − reviewsLastRefresh. That is the AGE OF THE COPY, which
### grows on a correct row that nobody has reviewed lately. You need both ends.


### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PART 2 · Does it track every kind of change?
### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Update the rating 5 → 2. Book should follow.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/reviews/{{reviewId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "rating": 2 }


### →  reviewsAvgRating: 2, reviewsCount: 1
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}


### Delete the only review. This is the interesting one:
### the aggregate now runs over ZERO rows. COUNT gives 0, AVG gives null.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/reviews/{{reviewId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}


### →  reviewsAvgRating: null, reviewsCount: 0  — back to the baseline.
### Not 0 and 0. If count comes back as something other than 0, the publisher
### is sending _sum where it means _count.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}


### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PART 3 · The ranked page  (use a SEEDED book — see header)
### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### The catalogue, best first. Unrated books sort last (NULLS LAST).
GET {{baseUrl}}/books?sort=top-rated&limit=10


### Page 2 of the same ordering — the ranking must be stable across pages.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books?sort=top-rated&page=2&limit=10


### Now review a seeded book highly, then re-run the first query and watch it
### climb. Paste a published book's id here:
@seededBookId = PASTE_A_PUBLISHED_BOOK_ID

POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "bookId": "{{seededBookId}}", "rating": 5, "comment": "Moves the ranking." }


### →  the book above should now be at or near the top, with nobody
###    having run a script.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books?sort=top-rated&limit=10


### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PART 4 · The proof that the buffer bought something
### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
###
### THIS IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS. It is the whole point of Lesson 08 and you
### have not seen it yet. Half of it is shell, half is here.
###
###   1.  docker compose stop book-service
###   2.  Send the three POSTs below. ALL THREE MUST RETURN 201.
###       Before the broker, this would have been three 503s — review-service
###       would have been down because book-service was down.
###   3.  Watch them pile up, unconsumed:
###         docker exec rabbitmq rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready consumers
###       Expect: reviews  3  0     ← three ready, ZERO consumers
###       This is rho = infinity on the curve. Note the window is now
###       unbounded and NOTHING has reported an error anywhere.
###   4.  docker compose start book-service
###   5.  Re-run the queue command until messages_ready is 0, then GET the
###       books below. All three ratings landed, unattended.
###
### AND WHILE IT DRAINS, capture mu: read the ack rate on the queue page in
### the management UI DURING the drain. That number is your consumer's
### capacity, and it is the only moment you can see it — in a system that is
### keeping up, the ack rate is just your arrival rate wearing a disguise.

### Three books to review while the consumer is down
# @name bookA
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{ "title": "Buffered A", "description": "Reviewed while the consumer was down.", "genre": ["test"] }

###

@bookAId = {{bookA.response.body.data.id}}

###
# @name bookB
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{ "title": "Buffered B", "description": "Reviewed while the consumer was down.", "genre": ["test"] }

###

@bookBId = {{bookB.response.body.data.id}}

###
# @name bookC
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{ "title": "Buffered C", "description": "Reviewed while the consumer was down.", "genre": ["test"] }

###

@bookCId = {{bookC.response.body.data.id}}


### ↓↓↓  NOW STOP BOOK-SERVICE, THEN SEND THESE THREE  ↓↓↓
### Each must return 201 with book-service down.

POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "bookId": "{{bookAId}}", "rating": 5, "comment": "Posted into the void." }

###
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "bookId": "{{bookBId}}", "rating": 4, "comment": "Posted into the void." }

###
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "bookId": "{{bookCId}}", "rating": 3, "comment": "Posted into the void." }


### ↓↓↓  NOW START BOOK-SERVICE, WAIT FOR THE QUEUE TO DRAIN  ↓↓↓
### All three must have landed.

GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookAId}}
###
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookBId}}
###
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookCId}}


### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PART 5 · What happens when the BROKER is down
### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
###
### Your decision 3 was: accept the write and log, repair via backfill.
### This is where you find out whether the code agrees with you.
###
###   1.  docker compose stop rabbitmq
###   2.  Send the POST below.
###
### WHAT SHOULD HAPPEN:  201. The review is saved. The rating is stale, and a
###                      line in the log says the event was not published.
### WHAT MUST NOT HAPPEN: a 500, or review-service exiting. An unawaited
###                      publish with no catch reaches unhandledRejection and
###                      takes the service down — which answers decision 3 as
###                      "take the whole service out", worse than any of the
###                      three options you were choosing between.
###
###   3.  docker compose logs review-service --tail 30
###   4.  docker compose start rabbitmq
###   5.  Repair:  npm run backfill:ratings   (or node scripts/backfill-ratings.js)
###       Then re-read the book. THE BACKFILL IS YOUR REPAIR PATH, not a
###       one-off migration. This is the step that proves it.

POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "bookId": "{{bookId}}", "rating": 4, "comment": "Written while the broker was down." }

###
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}


### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PART 6 · The poison message  (this is Build 2's fixture)
### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
###
### A review can outlive its book. The two tables are in different databases,
### so no foreign key can catch it. This is a REAL bug, not an injected one.
###
### Today it is silently discarded. After Build 2 it lands in reviews.dlq with
### x-first-death-reason telling you why.

### 1. A book, and a review of it
# @name doomedBook
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{ "title": "About To Be Deleted", "description": "Its review will outlive it.", "genre": ["test"] }

###

@doomedBookId = {{doomedBook.response.body.data.id}}

###
# @name doomedReview
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "bookId": "{{doomedBookId}}", "rating": 5, "comment": "Reviewing a doomed book." }

###

@doomedReviewId = {{doomedReview.response.body.data.id}}


### 2. Delete the BOOK. review-service has never heard of this.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/books/{{doomedBookId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}


### 3. Touch the review. review-service publishes happily; book-service tries
###    to update a row that is not there  →  Prisma P2025.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/reviews/{{doomedReviewId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "rating": 1 }


### 4. Now go look.
###    TODAY:        docker compose logs book-service --tail 30
###                  → the error is in a log nobody reads, message discarded.
###    AFTER BUILD 2: the message is in reviews.dlq. Management UI →
###                  Queues → reviews.dlq → Get messages → read
###                  x-first-death-reason and x-death.
###
###    AND CHECK THE QUEUE DID NOT BLOCK:
###      docker exec rabbitmq rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready
###    A normal review should still update its rating promptly. If messages_ready
###    is climbing and nothing moves, you have a hot loop, not a dead letter.
